# STACKIT Kubernetes Platform: **${platform_identifier}**

%{ if playground_mode ~}
> **Playground mode.** The platform identifier carries a random suffix so this deployment does not
> occupy a name for good, and the hosting project and tenant are left destroyable. Do not publish
> this platform or the building block definitions it registered to other workspaces. Redeploy with
> `playground_mode` set to false for a platform that is actually used.

%{ endif ~}
## Details

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Hosting Project** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](https://portal.stackit.cloud/projects/${stackit_project_id}) (`${stackit_project_id}`) |
| **SKE Cluster** | `${cluster_name}` — @buildingblock[${cluster_bb_uuid}] |
| **Platform Services** | @buildingblock[${platform_services_bb_uuid}] |
| **STACKIT Git** | %{ if forgejo_instance_url != null }[${forgejo_instance_url}](${forgejo_instance_url})%{ else }_provisioning_%{ endif } — @buildingblock[${git_bb_uuid}] |
| **meshStack Platform** | `${platform_identifier}` |

%{ if !forgejo_token_provided ~}
> [!WARNING]
> ## One step left: put a Forgejo token in

The platform is running, but the Git side of it is not wired up yet: the fresh Forgejo instance
carries no credential, and this building block does not mint one for itself yet (it could — see the
architecture README). Do this once, then update this building block:

1. Open the Forgejo instance at **${forgejo_instance_url_display}** and sign in with your STACKIT
   account.
2. Create the platform's **bot account** (or open the existing one). This account owns the
   organization and every repository application teams get, so use a shared platform-engineering
   identity, not a personal one.
3. As that bot account, go to **Settings → Applications → Access Tokens** and generate a token with
   these scopes:

   | Scope | Needed for |
   |---|---|
   | `write:organization` | creating the organization and the per-workspace teams inside it |
   | `write:repository` | creating repositories, their Actions secrets and variables, and dispatching the Actions workflow |
   | `read:user` | looking up workspace members by email to invite them to teams |

4. Copy the token — Forgejo shows it exactly once.
5. **Update this building block** and paste the token into the optional **Forgejo API Token**
   input. Optionally fill in the **Harbor** robot credentials at the same time, so application pods
   can pull private images from the shared STACKIT Harbor registry.

The next run creates the Forgejo organization and registers the building block definitions
application teams order. Nothing already provisioned is touched.

%{ else ~}
> [!TIP]
> ## Forgejo is wired up

The token was accepted, so the second phase ran. It created:

| What | Value |
|---|---|
| **Forgejo organization** | %{ if forgejo_organization != null }`${forgejo_organization}` in [${forgejo_instance_url}](${forgejo_instance_url})%{ else }_see the STACKIT Git building block_%{ endif } |
| **STACKIT Git Repository** definition | application teams can order a repository in that organization |
| **SKE Forgejo Connector** definition | wires a repository to a namespace on this cluster for Forgejo Actions CI/CD |
%{ if !harbor_configured ~}

> No Harbor robot credentials were supplied, so the connector creates an empty image-pull secret.
> Application pods can pull public images; add `harbor_username` and `harbor_password` to this
> building block to pull private ones from the shared STACKIT Harbor registry.
%{ endif ~}

%{ endif ~}
## What application teams get

The **${platform_identifier}** platform is published with a **dev** and a **prod** landing
zone. Application teams request a Kubernetes namespace on SKE by ordering a tenant in either
landing zone from the self-service catalog.
